          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em chain complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex"} complex
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            (often assumed to be an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
          %p
            The archetypical example, from which the name derives, is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+complex"} singular chain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Chain complexes are the basic objects of study in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
          %h3#basic Basic
          %p
            A
            %em chain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\bullet
            is a sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            (for instance
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{d_n : V_{n+1} \to V_n\}
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^2 = 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_n \circ d_{n+1} = 0
          %p
            A basic example is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+complex"} singular chain complex
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian group
            or similar. Both the first and the third of these types of example correspond, on the surface, to chain complexes in which the grading is by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            example can be included by indexing by the non-positive integers, but by defining them to take trivial, that is zero, values in other dimensions they become chain complexes in the sense used here. The more general definition is important as it is (i) more inclusive and (ii) leads to objects that behave well with respect to shift / translation operators, (see below).
          %p
            Chain complexes crucially come with their
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+maps"} chain maps
            between them that induce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            on these groups (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"}> quasi-isomorphisms
            ) as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MeaningInHomotopyTheory"} below
          %h3#MeaningInHomotopyTheory Meaning in homotopy theory
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            there is an equivalence between the category of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complex"} connective
              chain complexes of abelian groups
            and the category of
            = succeed "*" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                abelian simplicial groups
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/abelian+simplicial+groups"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NCC
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi AB
                    %mi Δ
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi AB
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NCC:AB^\Delta^{op}\to Ch_\bullet^+(AB)
          %p
            giving this equivalence is called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} normalized chain complex functor
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex functor
          %p
            In particular this reasoning shows that connective chain complexes of abelian groups are a model for abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-group"} ∞-groups
            (aka
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complexes
            with abelian group structure). This correspondence figures as part of the
            = succeed "-classification" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+cube"} cosmic cube
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-categories
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#InComponents In components
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              \-graded)
              %strong chain complex
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a collection of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mi n
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi ℤ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{C_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}
              %li
                %p
                  and of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n : C_n \to C_{n-1}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 3
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cdots \overset{\partial_3}{\to} C_2 \overset{\partial_2}{\to} C_1 \overset{\partial_1}{\to} C_0 \overset{\partial_0}{\to} C_{-1} \overset{\partial_{-1}}{\to} \cdots
            %p such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial_n  \circ \partial_{n+1} = 0
            %p
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of chain complexes is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
            (see there). Chain complexes with chain maps between them form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{C})
          %p
            One uses the following terminology for the components of a chain complex, all deriving from the example of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+complex"} singular chain complex
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet
              a chain complex
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the morphisms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n
                  are called the
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentials"} differentials
                  or
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                      maps
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element+in+an+abelian+category"} elements
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_n
                  are called the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      \-
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chains"} chains
              %li
                %p
                  the elements in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Z
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %mi ker
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        Z_n \coloneqq ker(\partial_{n-1})
                %p
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_{n-1} : C_n \to C_{n-1}
                  are called the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      \-
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
              %li
                %p
                  the elements in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi B
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %mi im
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        B_n \coloneqq im(\partial_n)
                %p
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_{n} : C_{n+1} \to C_{n}
                  are called the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                      \-
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
            %p
              Notice that due to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \partial = 0
              we have canonical inclusions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi n
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \hookrightarrow B_n \hookrightarrow Z_n \hookrightarrow C_n
                    \\,.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %msub
                          %mi Z
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msub
                          %mi B
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        H_n \coloneqq Z_n/B_n
                %p
                  is called the degree-
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mo •
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to B_n \to Z_n \to H_n \to 0
                    \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            notion:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a chain complex in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_n : V^n \to V^{n+1}
              increasing the degree.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              One also says
              %strong homologically graded
              complex, for the case that the differentials lower degree, and
              %em cohomologically graded
              complex for the case where they raise degree.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Frequently one also considers
              = succeed "-graded" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              = succeed ")" do
                %em nonnegatively graded
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
              = succeed "-graded" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_n=0
              when
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\lt 0
              = succeed "-graded" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              %em cochain
              complex is a cochain complex for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_n=0
              when
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\lt 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_n=0
              when
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\gt 0
          %h3#in_terms_of_translations In terms of translations
          %p
            Note that in particular, a chain complex is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded object
            with extra structure. This extra structure can be codified as a map of graded objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial:V\to T V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the ‘shift’ endofunctor of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr(C)
            of graded objects in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\partial) \circ \partial = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+translation"} with translation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : G \to G
            %strong chain complex
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+object"} differential object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_V : V \to T V
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi V
                  %mi T
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi T
                  %mi T
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \stackrel{\partial_V}{\to} T V \stackrel{T(\partial_V)}{\to} T T V
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi Gr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G= Gr(C)
            this recovers the original definition.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            Common choices for the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            include
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            and, generally, of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
          %h3#in_vector_spaces
            In
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _k
            a chain complex is also called a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+vector+space"} differential graded vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dg-algebra"} formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            (the latter considered as a chain complex with zero differentials). Nothing of the sort is true for chain complexes in more general categories.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
            of vector spaces carries the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            which makes it a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoids"} commutative monoids
            are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded-commutative+algebras"} differential graded-commutative algebras
          %h3#in_super_vector_spaces In super vector spaces
          %p
            A chain complex in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+spaces"} super vector spaces
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex+in+super+vector+spaces"} chain complex in super vector spaces
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            and the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoids"} commutative monoids
            are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded-commutative+superalgebras"} differential graded-commutative superalgebras
          %h3#in_chain_complexes In chain complexes
          %p
            A chain complex in a category of chain complexes is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
          %h3#singular_and_cellular_chain_complex Singular and cellular chain complex
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
          %p
            More generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \cdot R
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chains+on+a+simplicial+set"} chains on a simplicial set
          %h3#of_a_simplicial_abelian_group Of a simplicial abelian group
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\bullet
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(A)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternating+face+map+complex"} alternating face map complex
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\bullet(A)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normalized+chain+complex"} normalized chain complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            says that this construction establishes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between non-negatively-graded chain complexes and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+groups"} simplicial abelian groups
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#model_structure Model structure
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch(A)
            of chain complexes in an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
          %p
            There is another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} Hurewicz model structure on chain complexes
            whose homotopy category is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+chain+complexes"} homotopy category of chain complexes
